const BIO_U4T1: SeedCard[] = [
  { complexity: "complex_familiar", front: "Explain how a single base-pair change can have anything from no effect to a catastrophic effect on the resulting polypeptide. (3 marks)", back: "1 mark: A point substitution may be silent — the genetic code is degenerate, so the new codon can specify the same amino acid; the polypeptide is unchanged. 1 mark: A missense substitution changes one amino acid — effect depends on position and chemistry (a swap in the active site or a folding-critical residue can destroy function; elsewhere, minimal effect); a nonsense substitution creates a premature stop codon, truncating the polypeptide. 1 mark: If the change is an insertion/deletion of one base, it causes a frameshift — every codon downstream is re-read, producing a garbled sequence and usually an early stop, which is why frameshifts are typically far more damaging than substitutions." },
  { complexity: "complex_familiar", front: "Explain how gene expression is regulated by chromatin structure and transcription factors, and why this enables cell differentiation. (3 marks)", back: "1 mark: Chemical tags (e.g. on histones/DNA) alter chromatin packing — heterochromatin is tightly packed and transcriptionally silent; euchromatin is open and available for transcription — so tagging switches regions on or off without changing the sequence. 1 mark: Transcription factors bind the promoter region of a gene and increase or block RNA polymerase recruitment, giving gene-by-gene control in response to environmental/developmental signals. 1 mark: Differentiation: every body cell holds the same genome; cells become different types because each expresses a different subset — regulation, not gene content, defines a neuron vs a skin cell (with HOX-family transcription factors regulating body-plan morphology)." },
  { complexity: "complex_unfamiliar", front: "A couple's first child has an autosomal recessive condition neither parent shows. The father's new DNA profile from gel electrophoresis is then found to not match the child at several loci. Interpret BOTH findings genetically, including what each technique can and cannot conclude, and resolve the apparent contradiction. (5 marks)", back: "Model response: Finding 1 — the inheritance logic: an affected child with unaffected parents fits autosomal recessive inheritance with both parents heterozygous carriers (Aa × Aa → 1/4 aa affected); a Punnett square supports carrier parents without either showing the phenotype. Finding 2 — the DNA profile: PCR amplifies selected variable regions and gel electrophoresis separates the fragments by size; a child's bands must each be attributable to one parent, so multiple non-matching loci indicate the tested man is very unlikely to be the biological father (band patterns are characteristic because individuals differ in these region lengths). Resolving the contradiction: the recessive analysis ASSUMED both social parents are the genetic parents; the profile removes that assumption — the biological father (whoever he is) must be the second carrier, while the tested man's carrier status is now irrelevant to this child. What each technique cannot conclude: the pedigree/Punnett logic cannot verify parentage, only consistency with a model; the profile cannot detect the condition itself (those loci are not the disease gene) — it establishes relatedness only. Together they show conclusions are only as strong as their assumptions. Marking points: (1) correct recessive/carrier explanation; (2) correct reading of profile exclusion; (3) PCR + electrophoresis roles described; (4) contradiction resolved via the parentage assumption; (5) limits of each technique explicitly stated." },
];

