const PSY_U4T3: SeedCard[] = [
  { complexity: "complex_familiar", front: "Explain how cognitive dissonance theory accounts for people changing their ATTITUDES after behaving against them, and why a SMALL reward produces more attitude change than a large one (Festinger & Carlsmith, 1959). (3 marks)", back: "1 mark: Discrepancy between attitude and behaviour ('I think it's boring' vs 'I told someone it was fun') creates uncomfortable dissonance and a drive to reduce it; the behaviour is done, so the attitude shifts to match it. 1 mark: A large reward ($20) provides external justification — 'I said it for the money' — so no dissonance remains and the attitude doesn't change. 1 mark: A small reward ($1) can't justify the counter-attitudinal act, so participants reduce dissonance internally by deciding the task really was enjoyable — challenging learning theory's prediction that bigger rewards produce bigger change." },
  { complexity: "complex_unfamiliar", front: "A new student from a rival school joins your class. Within a week, classmates describe her as 'arrogant' after she sits alone at lunch. Using social identity theory, attribution concepts (including the fundamental attribution error), and stereotypes, analyse how this judgement formed — and predict what would reduce it. (5 marks)", back: "Model response: Social identity theory: classmates categorise her as out-group ('rival school') via social categorisation, identify with their own school group, and engage in social comparison favouring the in-group — priming negative interpretation of her behaviour before any real evidence. Attribution: sitting alone is explained dispositionally ('arrogant') rather than situationally (knows nobody, anxious, no established group to join) — the fundamental attribution error: observers underweight situational causes of others' behaviour. The rival-school stereotype supplies the specific content ('students from there think they're better'), and confirmation bias then maintains it: ambiguous behaviours are noticed when they fit and ignored when they don't. Prediction: contact that recategorises her into a shared in-group would reduce the judgement — e.g. placing her in a cooperative group task where the class and she share a goal (a superordinate identity, 'our team'), plus firsthand situational information (learning she knows no one) that makes the situational attribution available. Individual friendships cutting across the group boundary would further erode the stereotype's usefulness. Marking points: (1) all three SIT processes applied; (2) FAE correctly identified with the situational alternative stated; (3) stereotype + confirmation bias as content and maintenance; (4) prediction uses recategorisation/shared goals; (5) coherent mechanism from group membership → interpretation → judgement, not a list of terms." },
];

